+Snapshot validation, backend preparation, and lookup all belong to this node. The lookup backend uses a local embedded SQLite index built from each validated snapshot. No certificate behavior or interface change is part of that selection.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|
+| Lookup backend | Local embedded SQLite index | Predictable lookups with no service to run; index rebuild stays inside registry component; satisfies 250 ms goal on 4 GB RAM for 90k records; avoids network dependency and recurring cost. |
+| Index rebuild strategy | Rebuild on each valid snapshot import | Simpler than incremental updates; import is infrequent; 150 MB snapshot rebuilds well within kiosk resources; atomic swap avoids partial state. |
 
 ## Snapshot Import
 
 Verify the signature using a bundled public key, reject unsupported schema versions, and validate record serial numbers for uniqueness. Prepare the new snapshot without exposing partially imported records. Activate it only after all validation succeeds. An import failure leaves the previous snapshot active and reports a useful error.
 
+Build the SQLite index from the validated records as part of import, then atomically swap the active database file. The index schema stores serial number (primary key), calibration date, due date, description, and the snapshot date as a global property.
+
 ## Lookup Behavior
 
-Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The concrete index and query path remain open pending the user's choice.
+Normalize surrounding whitespace and uppercase ASCII letters before lookup. Serial-number punctuation remains significant. Return the snapshot date even when no matching record exists. The SQLite index provides point lookups by normalized serial number.
